```

That is correct for the catalog callers (`spec.name` sites in `provider_commands.dart:217,866,1567`, `provider_models.dart:421,469`, `agent_cli_provider_presets.dart:231`) and for `_fetchSavedEntryModels` (`provider_models.dart:561`, keyed by `entry.name`). But the **active-provider** refresh passes `model.provider` — and for a custom entry `model.provider` is the **catalog spec name** (`buildCatalogModel(spec.name, …)` in `_switchProvider`), not the entry name the `providers:` binding is keyed by:

```dart
// provider_models.dart:787-789
Future<List<String>> _fetchActiveProviderModels(Model model) {
  return _fetchProviderModelIds(model.provider, model.baseUrl, _apiKey);
}
```

Reachable flow (verified at HEAD): bind `providers.kimi_me.network` → `/provider kimi_me` → `_switchToSavedProvider` correctly proxies the **chat** stream via `_activeCustomName`, then fires `unawaited(_refreshModelCache())` (`provider_commands.dart:2310`) → `_fetchActiveProviderModels` → `_fetchProviderModelIds('openai', 'https://api.…', key)` → `modelFetchClient('openai')` → `providerNetworks['openai']` is null → **shared direct client → the bound provider's `/models` request (Authorization header and all) leaves directly**. `_modelCacheFresh` is never cleared on switch (only added to, `provider_models.dart:67`), so the fetch runs on every first refresh of the process. This is the same silent fail-open class as the stream-side gate bug, on the mainline model-list flow that OQ2 explicitly promises honors the gate ("model-list and quota requests honor the same gate").

**Fix**: key the active-provider refresh on the same identity the stream bind uses — `_activeCustomName ?? model.provider` (mirroring `provider_models.dart:700`'s `activeName` resolution) — or resolve the identity in one place and hand it to both gates. The zero-egress IT only exercises the stream; a models-refresh probe for a bound custom entry would have caught this.

⚠️ **IMPORTANT (carried — unresolved at head `e5437ced55`): `proxyPasswordEnv` without `proxyUsername` parses but the credential is silently never used**

The validation enforces one direction only:

```dart
if (username != null && passwordEnv == null) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: "proxyUsername" without "proxyPasswordEnv" — the '
    'password must be referenced by env name, never inline',
  );
}
```

The mirror case — `proxyPasswordEnv` set, `proxyUsername` omitted — is accepted at parse time, but the password is then dead config:

- http/https: `_wireProxyBasicAuth` returns immediately when `username == null` (`proxy_connector_io.dart:110`), so no credentials are ever registered.
- socks5: `useAuth = username != null` (`proxy_connector_io.dart`), so the greeting offers only `0x00` (no-auth) and the RFC1929 exchange never happens.

The user's intent is unambiguous (they configured a password), the config passes strict validation, and the failure surfaces later as a proxy-side 407 / RFC1929 rejection — which is fail-closed, but the error text then points the user at `proxyPasswordEnv`, which they believe they already set. Please confirm the intent; if asymmetric config is unintended, reject it in the same block:

```dart
if (passwordEnv != null && username == null) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: "proxyPasswordEnv" without "proxyUsername" — both are '
    'required (HTTP Basic / SOCKS5 RFC1929 send them as a pair)',
  );
}
```

(If anonymous+password proxies are a real thing you want to support, at least reject it for `socks5`, where RFC1929 strictly requires both fields.)

💡 **SUGGESTION (carried — unresolved at head `e5437ced55`): validate the proxy port range at parse time**

`ProviderNetworkConfig.fromYaml` validates scheme, credentials, and host, but not the port: `proxy: http://proxy.corp:99999` parses fine (`Uri.port` accepts >65535) and only fails later at `Socket.connect`/`SecureSocket.connect` with a raw `ArgumentError`/`SocketException` — the fail-closed wrapper will render it, but the message won't name the config key the way the other parse errors do. One line in the factory (next to the host check below) keeps all config errors equally actionable:

```dart
if (uri.port != 0 && (uri.port < 1 || uri.port > 65535)) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: proxy port ${uri.port} is out of range 1-65535 (got: "$rawProxy")',
  );
}
```

Add the >65535 case to the existing scheme/host rejection UT for symmetry.

💡 **SUGGESTION: RFC1929 credentials are `ascii.encode`d — a non-ASCII or >255-byte credential fails with a cryptic error (or a RangeError) at request time instead of a named config error at bind time**

```dart
final user = ascii.encode(username);
final pass = ascii.encode(password ?? '');
socket.add([0x01, user.length, ...user, pass.length, ...pass]);
```

Two edge cases:

1. `ascii.encode` **throws** on any code point > 127 — a password with a single non-ASCII character surfaces (through the fail-closed wrapper, at least) as `FormatException: … not in the range 0..127` inside the actionable text instead of a config-style error naming `proxyPasswordEnv`.
2. RFC1929 caps ULEN/PLEN at 255 (one length byte each): a 256+ byte username/password makes `socket.add` throw a `RangeError` mid-handshake.

Neither is a security hole (both fail closed, no direct egress), but both are exactly the class of misconfiguration the parse-time validators exist to name. Cheap fix: validate at bind time in `_Socks5ProxyConnector.createClient` (or `socks5Bind`) —

```dart
if (username!.codeUnits.any((c) => c > 127) ||
    (password ?? '').codeUnits.any((c) => c > 127)) {
  throw ConfigException('$label: proxyUsername/proxyPasswordEnv must be ASCII for SOCKS5 RFC1929');
}
```

— or switch to `utf8.encode` (the common server-side interpretation) and cap/validate the 255-byte lengths explicitly.

