arguments.content:
🚨 **BLOCKING: every CodeMie custom-entry chat bind keys the CATALOG identity (`'openai'`), not the saved entry name — a bound CodeMie entry's chat traffic (JWT/cookie credential on the wire) rides direct**

The round-3 rework fixed the *fetch* face of this (thread 12 → `_codeMieGateIdentity` + threaded `providerName:`), but the *chat-stream* face still keys the wrong identity. Both switch helpers bind the catalog spec name:

```dart
// provider_codemie_sso.dart:190 (_switchCodeMieProvider — SSO cookie flow)
_streamFunction = _catalogStreamFunction(
  spec.kind,
  '',
  providerName: spec.name,   // <-- catalog identity, never the entry name
);
```

and the same `providerName: spec.name` sits in `_switchCodeMieJwtProvider` (`codemie_provider_commands.dart:354`). At every call site `spec` is the **catalog openai spec**, never the entry:

- `_applyCodeMieSsoCredentials` (`provider_codemie_sso.dart:120`): `final spec = providerCatalog['openai']!` — and it sets `_activeCustomName = name` (the user-chosen entry name) three lines **before** the call;
- `_applyCodeMieJwtCredentials` (`codemie_provider_commands.dart:317`): identical shape;
- `/provider codemie` re-switch (`codemie_provider_commands.dart:409/429`): passes `entry.spec` — and `CustomProviderEntry.spec => resolveCliProviderSpec(apiType)` is the **openai catalog spec** for every CodeMie entry (`apiType: 'openai'`);
- wizard `_switchSetupEntry` CodeMie branches (`provider_commands.dart:354/363`): passes `setup.spec` — same catalog spec.

Reachable flow (all at head `e2fb7b909c`): bind `providers.corp.network:` → `/provider codemie sso` (or jwt) → entry saved as `corp`, `_activeCustomName = 'corp'` → the chat stream binds `proxiedProviderClient('openai')` → `providerNetworks['openai']` is null → `sharedProviderHttpClient()` → **the bound entry's chat request — carrying the JWT Bearer / SSO cookie — leaves directly, silently**, while the very next `_refreshModelCache()` keys `_activeCustomName ?? model.provider` = `'corp'` and rides the proxy. Chat direct + models proxied is the worst split: the credential egress is exactly the part AC4 promises never goes direct. The automatic mid-session refresh path (`_handleCodeMieSsoCommand` → `_applyCodeMieSsoCredentials`, run with the binding and entry already in place) is thread 12's original scenario — this is the same bug one layer down.

**Fix**: thread the entry identity into both helpers (a required `providerName` argument, exactly like `_catalogStreamFunction` now requires one): `name` from the two apply sites, `entry.name` from the `/provider codemie` re-switch branches, `entry?.name` from `_switchSetupEntry`. An IT that SSO-connects a bound fake entry and asserts zero direct egress on the chat stream would lock it.

💡 **SUGGESTION (carried — unresolved at head `e2fb7b909c`): validate the proxy port range at parse time**

`ProviderNetworkConfig.fromYaml` validates scheme, credentials, and host, but not the port: `proxy: http://proxy.corp:99999` parses fine (`Uri.port` accepts >65535) and only fails later at `Socket.connect`/`SecureSocket.connect` with a raw `ArgumentError`/`SocketException` — the fail-closed wrapper will render it, but the message won't name the config key the way the other parse errors do. One line in the factory (next to the host check below) keeps all config errors equally actionable:

```dart
if (uri.port != 0 && (uri.port < 1 || uri.port > 65535)) {
  throw ConfigException(
    '$configLabel: proxy port ${uri.port} is out of range 1-65535 (got: "$rawProxy")',
  );
}
```

Add the >65535 case to the existing scheme/host rejection UT for symmetry.

💡 **SUGGESTION (carried — unresolved at head `e2fb7b909c`, now in `socks5NegotiateAuth` after the CRAP refactor): RFC1929 credentials are `ascii.encode`d — a non-ASCII or >255-byte credential fails with a cryptic error (or a RangeError) at request time instead of a named config error at bind time**

```dart
final user = ascii.encode(username);
final pass = ascii.encode(password ?? '');
socket.add([0x01, user.length, ...user, pass.length, ...pass]);
```

Two edge cases:

1. `ascii.encode` **throws** on any code point > 127 — a password with a single non-ASCII character surfaces (through the fail-closed wrapper, at least) as `FormatException: … not in the range 0..127` inside the actionable text instead of a config-style error naming `proxyPasswordEnv`.
2. RFC1929 caps ULEN/PLEN at 255 (one length byte each): a 256+ byte username/password makes `socket.add` throw a `RangeError` mid-handshake.

Neither is a security hole (both fail closed, no direct egress), but both are exactly the class of misconfiguration the parse-time validators exist to name. Cheap fix: validate in `socks5NegotiateAuth` (or at bind time in `_Socks5ProxyConnector.createClient`) —

```dart
if (username.codeUnits.any((c) => c > 127) ||
    (password ?? '').codeUnits.any((c) => c > 127)) {
  throw ConfigException('$label: proxyUsername/proxyPasswordEnv must be ASCII for SOCKS5 RFC1929');
}
```

— or switch to `utf8.encode` (the common server-side interpretation) and cap/validate the 255-byte lengths explicitly.

